various yath commands, liek `yath test` and `yath start` will star harness services.

The yath command will queue runs either by sending an IPC message (using a
queue run interface on the handle returned from starting the harness), or by
including it in the construction parameters for the harness before it starts
the service. You can also queue a "stop here" message that indicates the
service should shut itself down after the queued run(s) are complete. This can
also be send during construction args to indicate it should shut down when all
runs provided at construction are complete.

When a command like `yath test` is used, the command should initialize
IPC::Manager BEFORE crating the harness service, and pass the ipc info to the
harness, in these cases the command process will become the IPC parent, with
the handle returned from spawning the harness service having the IPC id for the
command process. The command should use the handle to interact with the
service, and IPC messages between the service and the command use that IPC
channel.

The harness will spawn any resource services for any top level resources that
define them. It will also spawn a run service for each run it starts once it
gets to it. The resource services and run services have the harness service as
their IPC parent in their collectors. The collectors should send IPC messages
to their parents indicating any logger artifacts they will produce, as well as
a final exit message indicating they have exited, and what the exit code was.

Each run service will spawn any resource services associated with resources
exclusive to the run. The run will then be the IPC parent for those services.
The run service will also spawn the test jobs and will be the IPC parent for
those. An IPC parent should always point t the parent service, not the paren
service's collector. The one exception is the harness service which points at
its handle in the process that spawned it, or in some cases is undefined.

A test job itself does not have an IPC handle, but its collector does, it
should have 3 IPC id's it knows ot send messages to:
    1) harness - Should be able to send ipc messages directly to the harness service
    2) run - Should be able to send ipc messages directly to the run service
    3) parent - Should always be able to send messages ot its parent
    Note) Current the parent and the run services are always the same ID, but with preloading they can be different.
    Note) The run service id should always be the run_id of the run, so it can be passed along that way

Flow:

A test job generates output to stdout and stderr which is collected by the test
job's collector. The collector passes the output through a parser, then an
auditor, and though any loggers. When the test job is complete the test jobs
collector will then notify its run service that the job is complete, it will
include pass/fail status, exit code, and start + end times (hi-res) of the test
job. It will send the same IPC message to the main harness directly via IPC.
This will be a 'test job complete' message. These messages are used for the
scheduler in the main harness to know when a job is done to free up resources.
It is also used by the run service to accumulate pass, fail, and duration data.

The run services start any resource services that are exclusive to the job, and
spawn any tests for the job. It also recieves IPC messages from the test job
collectors, such as 'test job started' and 'test job completed' messages. Any
event it recieves should also be echoed to STDOUT/STDERR as en event for the
services collector to pass through the parser and any loggers. When a run
service is supposed to close (run complete) it should send an IPC to the main
harness service with the aggregated results (overall pass/fail, how many tests
passed and failed, how long the run ran, individual test job pass/fail/times)

The harness service manages global resources and run services. It also is the
scheduler that decides when a run should start, and when tests inside the run
should start. When it decides a test should start (based on if the run is
started, and if the resources are available) it sends an IPC message to the
correct service to spawn the test. Usually 'run the test' messages will go to
the run service, but in a preload situation it may be to a preload service
process.

IPC messages are never sent to collectors, only to services. IPC collectors can
send messages to services, just not recieve them. Collectors never send
requests, only messages which require no response. Collectors and Services need
distinct IPC identities, generated UUIDs are fine for collector IPC ids.
Services need IPC handles that match their purpose, run services use the run
id, resource services can be the service type + a uuid, or be provided with a
unique name/id during construction by the resources. The harness should usually
be 'harness', but downstream IPC clients should not hardcode it, it should be
passed to them because it will be possible (but unlikely) to have multiple
harnesses share an IPC bus.

By default no loggers are used, the IPC described above should cover getting
the essential data back to the `yath test` and similar commands so that it can
exit true or false depending on test status.

When loggers are active then each collector should initialize all applicable
loggers from the list. `yath test` should by default enable the JSON and JSONL
loggers, which all services and test jobs should support. All collectors should
accept regular stderr/stdout as well as event bursts via their Atomic::Pipes.
Test jobs should use the Stream2 formatter to make sure their output is what
the collector expects, but the collector should also gracefully accept direct
TAP from older producers.

Collectors should send IPC messages (maybe reuse some already described) when
they start and stop that indicate which loggers are being used, and what
artifacts they are expected to produce. Artifacts are usually files on disk,
but may also be things like uploads to a database. These events should go to
the run if the collector is associated with a run (run resources, test jobs).
They should go to the harness service if they are not associated with a job.
Preload notifications should propogate to parent services until they eventually
reach the harness or a run. By propogate it means: preload-child-collector ->
preload-parent -> preload-parent-collector (via event burst over stderr/stdout)
-> root-preload-service -> root-preload-service-collector (via stderr/stdout
event burst) -> run-service or harness-service

We want to convey test state, as well as artifacts (from loggers) up to the
run, or for services with no run up to the main harness. But we do not want to
send all events generated by a specific test, those instead go to artifacts
through loggers, or optionally are completely dropped if there are no loggers.

NOTE: The workdir hould be created by the `yath test` or similar commands and
passed to the harness at construction, along with the name of the logs
directory (default logs).

When we get to renderers, they will be running from the `yath test` command
process which will have the workdir and logdir that were passed into the
harness. They renderer will be able to look at the harness service artifact(s)
by service name (usually harness, but also passed into the harness at
construction). They can also look for any artifacts for the run by run-id. If
there are no artifacts then the renderers have nothing to render until the job
is done when they can provide pass/fail info requested from the harness
service. If there are global and/or run specific artifacts (json and jsonl
files) then it should locate them and render events from them.
